{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-556440",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate $\\sqrt{29},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $29.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<29<36,$ we know that the square root of $29$ lies between $5$ and $6.$ This tells us that $4.9$ and $14.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $29$ is much closer to $25$ than to $36$ (the distance to $25$ is $4,$ and the distance to $36$ is $7$), we expect $\\sqrt{29}$ to be near the lower end of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.7}^{2} & =32.49 \\\\ {5.9}^{2} & =34.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${5.4}^{2}=29.16$ is very close to $29.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}$ is $5.4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
